The Security Council,

Recalling its previous resolutions and the statements of its President concerning the Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC), in particular resolutions 2765 (2024), 2773 (2025), 2783 (2025) and presidential statement S/PRST/2023/5,

Having considered the report of the Secretary-General on the United Nations Organization Stabilization Mission in the Democratic Republic of the Congo (MONUSCO) (S/2025/779) and taking note of its recommendations,

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of the DRC as well as all States in the region, and emphasizing the urgent need to respect fully the principles of non-interference, good-neighbourliness and regional cooperation,

Reaffirming the basic principles of peacekeeping, including consent of the parties, impartiality and non-use of force except in self-defence and defence of the mandate,

Reaffirming that the Government of the DRC bears the primary responsibility to protect civilians within its territory and subject to its jurisdiction, including from international crimes, and underlining the importance of national efforts to restore State authority throughout the country,

Stressing that there can be no military solution to the conflict in the eastern DRC and that sustainable peace requires inclusive dialogue, respect for the sovereignty and territorial integrity of the DRC and full implementation of regional peace processes,

Welcoming the signing of the peace agreement between the DRC and Rwanda in Washington, D.C., on 4 December 2025, the agreement reached in Doha on 14 October 2025 to establish an oversight body towards a permanent ceasefire, the Regional Economic Integration Framework agreed on 7 November 2025 and the earlier ceasefire and verification arrangements, and calling for their full and expeditious implementation,

Welcoming the announcement by the March 23 Movement (M23) on 16 December 2025 that it will withdraw from Uvira following a request from the United States, while expressing grave concern that M23 continues to occupy Congolese territory and has captured Sange and Uvira, causing massive displacement, including more than 20,000 people fleeing across the Burundi–DRC border, and demanding the immediate, full and verifiable withdrawal of M23 from all occupied areas and the cessation of all offensive operations,

Expressing grave concern at the continued violence and violations of international humanitarian law and human rights law by all parties, particularly armed groups, including M23, the Allied Democratic Forces (ADF), CODECO, the Forces démocratiques de libération du Rwanda (FDLR), Mai-Mai groups and other domestic and foreign armed groups, and condemning in particular the attacks in Komanda on 27 July 2025 and in Lubero on 14 November 2025, as well as all attacks on civilians, hospitals, medical facilities, humanitarian personnel, schools and camps for internally displaced persons and refugees,

Condemning the continued operational support delivered by external forces from a neighbouring State to M23, as reported by the Group of Experts, and the unauthorized presence in the eastern DRC of such forces in a manner inconsistent with the sovereignty and territorial integrity of the DRC, and expressing concern at reports of the deployment of sophisticated weaponry, including surface-to-air missiles, and of GPS jamming and spoofing activities that undermine MONUSCO’s ability to implement its mandate and threaten the safety of peacekeepers, civil aviation and humanitarian actors,

Expressing grave concern at the deteriorating humanitarian situation, with an estimated 25.4 million Congolese in need of humanitarian assistance, approximately 6.5 million internally displaced persons, 527,000 refugees in the DRC and more than 1.1 million refugees from the DRC elsewhere in Africa, and demanding safe, rapid, unhindered and sustained humanitarian access to all civilians in need in accordance with humanitarian principles,

Condemning all violations and abuses of human rights, including conflict-related sexual violence and the recruitment and use of children, and stressing the need to end impunity and ensure accountability for crimes under international law,

Welcoming the declaration by the DRC on 1 December 2025 that the Ebola outbreak in Kasaï Province has ended, and recognizing the adverse effects of climate change, ecological changes and natural disasters on the stability of the DRC,

Reaffirming the important role of women and youth in the prevention, management and resolution of conflicts and in peacebuilding, and stressing the importance of their full, equal, meaningful and safe participation in all efforts for the maintenance and promotion of peace and security in the DRC, including in regional-led processes,

Recalling the sanctions regime established by resolutions 1493 (2003), 1533 (2004) and 1807 (2008) and extended by resolution 2783 (2025), and emphasizing the importance of full implementation of the arms embargo and other measures,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Decides to extend the mandate of MONUSCO until 20 December 2026;

2. Decides that MONUSCO shall continue to implement its mandate through a phased, flexible and conditions-based approach, in close coordination with the Government of the DRC and in full respect of the basic principles of peacekeeping, prioritizing the protection of civilians, support for the implementation of ceasefire and peace agreements, facilitation of safe and unhindered humanitarian access, human rights and international humanitarian law monitoring and reporting, and support to stabilization and the restoration of State authority, and that the Mission’s withdrawal shall be conditions-based and shall avoid creating security gaps;

3. Requests MONUSCO to use all means at its disposal to protect civilians, including in Uvira, Sange, Goma, Bukavu and other threatened areas, and to support the safe, timely and unhindered delivery of humanitarian assistance to all civilians in need;

4. Demands that all armed groups immediately cease hostilities, end all offensive operations and withdraw from all occupied areas, including the immediate, full and verifiable withdrawal of M23 from Uvira and Sange, and calls for the full implementation of ceasefire and peace agreements;

5. Demands the immediate withdrawal of all external forces from the territory of the DRC, including Rwandan forces as reported by the Group of Experts, without further delay, consistent with the sovereignty and territorial integrity of the DRC, and calls upon all States to refrain from providing military support to armed groups and to respect fully the arms embargo;

6. Welcomes the announcement by M23 of its intention to withdraw from Uvira and demands its immediate and faithful implementation, and further demands that M23 and all other armed groups release all children and abducted persons, cease attacks against civilians and humanitarian personnel, and allow unhindered humanitarian access;

7. Calls upon the DRC and Rwanda to implement fully and in good faith the peace agreement signed in Washington, D.C., on 4 December 2025, the Regional Economic Integration Framework of 7 November 2025, the Doha agreement of 14 October 2025 and the Luanda and Nairobi processes, including through the neutralization of the FDLR, the withdrawal of external forces, the establishment and effective functioning of oversight and verification mechanisms and the pursuit of durable political solutions;

8. Expresses its full support for the mediation and facilitation efforts of the United States, Angola, Qatar, the African Union, the International Conference on the Great Lakes Region, the Southern African Development Community and the United Nations, and encourages the continuation and coordination of those efforts;

9. Demands that all parties respect and protect civilians, humanitarian personnel, medical personnel, hospitals and other medical facilities, schools and camps for internally displaced persons and refugees, in accordance with their obligations under international humanitarian law and international human rights law, and stresses the need to ensure accountability for all violations and abuses, including conflict-related sexual violence and the recruitment and use of children;

10. Calls for the full, equal, meaningful and safe participation of women and youth in peace negotiations, political processes, stabilization efforts and decision-making related to the DRC and the Great Lakes region, and calls upon all actors to strengthen the protection of children and support their rehabilitation and reintegration;

11. Condemns the illicit exploitation and trade of natural resources and all violations of the arms embargo and sanctions regime, calls upon all States to implement fully the measures of the sanctions regime and to cooperate with the Group of Experts, and emphasizes that the sanctions regime and the Group of Experts remain essential tools to address violence and destabilization in the eastern DRC;

12. Urges Member States and international partners to provide adequate humanitarian assistance, support the equitable sharing of the burden and responsibility for hosting refugees, and provide support to the DRC in strengthening its defence and security forces, State authority, rule of law and security sector reform, in line with the primary responsibility of the Government of the DRC to protect civilians;

13. Requests the Secretary-General to continue to report to the Council every three months, with the first report by 20 March 2026, on the implementation of this resolution, including the security situation in the eastern DRC, the implementation of ceasefire and peace agreements, humanitarian access and the humanitarian situation, the protection of civilians, human rights and international humanitarian law, and the implementation of MONUSCO’s conditions-based withdrawal, and to include recommendations for the adaptation of the Mission’s mandate as appropriate;

14. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

