Welcoming the announcement by the March 23 Movement (M23) on 16 December 2025 that it will withdraw from Uvira following a request from the United States, while expressing grave concern that M23 continues to occupy Congolese territory and has captured Sange and Uvira, causing massive displacement, including more than 20,000 people fleeing across the Burundi–DRC border, and demanding the immediate, full and verifiable withdrawal of M23 from all occupied areas and the cessation of all offensive operations,

Expressing grave concern at the continued violence and violations of international humanitarian law and human rights law by all parties, particularly armed groups, including M23, the Allied Democratic Forces (ADF), CODECO, the Forces démocratiques de libération du Rwanda (FDLR), Mai-Mai groups and other domestic and foreign armed groups, and condemning in particular the attacks in Komanda on 27 July 2025 and in Lubero on 14 November 2025, as well as all attacks on civilians, hospitals, medical facilities, humanitarian personnel, schools and camps for internally displaced persons and refugees,

Expressing grave concern at reports, as documented by the Group of Experts, of continued operational support to M23, including by the Rwanda Defence Force, and of the unauthorized presence in the eastern DRC of Rwandan forces, in a manner inconsistent with the sovereignty and territorial integrity of the DRC, while stressing that such reports should be verified through the agreed regional mechanisms and respecting the parties’ commitments under the Washington, Doha and Luanda processes, and expressing concern at reports of the deployment of sophisticated weaponry, including surface-to-air missiles, and of GPS jamming and spoofing activities that undermine MONUSCO’s ability to implement its mandate and threaten the safety of peacekeepers, civil aviation and humanitarian actors,

Expressing grave concern at the deteriorating humanitarian situation, with an estimated 25.4 million Congolese in need of humanitarian assistance, approximately 6.5 million internally displaced persons, 527,000 refugees in the DRC and more than 1.1 million refugees from the DRC elsewhere in Africa, and demanding safe, rapid, unhindered and sustained humanitarian access to all civilians in need in accordance with humanitarian principles,

Condemning all violations and abuses of human rights, including conflict-related sexual violence and the recruitment and use of children, and stressing the need to end impunity and ensure accountability for crimes under international law,

Welcoming the declaration by the DRC on 1 December 2025 that the Ebola outbreak in Kasaï Province has ended, and recognizing the adverse effects of climate change, ecological changes and natural disasters on the stability of the DRC,

Reaffirming the important role of women and youth in the prevention, management and resolution of conflicts and in peacebuilding, and stressing the importance of their full, equal, meaningful and safe participation in all efforts for the maintenance and promotion of peace and security in the DRC, including in regional-led processes,

Recalling the sanctions regime established by resolutions 1493 (2003), 1533 (2004) and 1807 (2008) and extended by resolution 2783 (2025), and emphasizing the importance of full implementation of the arms embargo and other measures,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Decides to extend the mandate of MONUSCO until 20 December 2026;

2. Decides that MONUSCO shall continue to implement its mandate through a phased, flexible and conditions-based approach, in close coordination with, and with the consent of, the Government of the DRC, in consultation with local communities, including women, youth and civil society, and in accordance with the Government’s priorities and the joint MONUSCO–DRC comprehensive disengagement plan, and in full respect of the basic principles of peacekeeping, prioritizing the protection of civilians, support for the implementation of ceasefire and peace agreements, facilitation of safe and unhindered humanitarian access, human rights and international humanitarian law monitoring and reporting, and support to stabilization and the restoration of State authority, and that the Mission’s withdrawal shall be conditions-based and shall avoid creating security gaps;

3. Requests MONUSCO to use all means at its disposal to protect civilians, including in Uvira, Sange, Goma, Bukavu, Ituri and other threatened areas, to support the safe, timely and unhindered delivery of humanitarian assistance to all civilians in need, and to take all necessary measures to ensure the safety and security of MONUSCO personnel, in accordance with the Mission’s mandate;

4. Demands that all parties cooperate fully with MONUSCO, ensure the safety, security and freedom of movement of its personnel and assets, allow safe, rapid and unhindered access for the Mission and humanitarian actors, and refrain from any action that threatens peacekeepers or impedes the Mission in implementing its mandate;

5. Demands that all armed groups immediately cease hostilities, end all offensive operations and withdraw from all occupied areas, and demands the immediate, full and verifiable withdrawal of M23 from all occupied areas, including Uvira and Sange, to be monitored and verified by MONUSCO and regional verification mechanisms, and calls for the full implementation of ceasefire and peace agreements;

6. Demands the full and verifiable withdrawal of all unauthorized external forces from the territory of the Democratic Republic of the Congo, including, as reported by the Group of Experts, the Rwanda Defence Force, in accordance with the agreed timelines and verification mechanisms under the Washington, Doha and merged Luanda and Nairobi processes, consistent with the sovereignty and territorial integrity of the Democratic Republic of the Congo; demands that Rwanda immediately cease all military support to M23; calls upon all States to refrain from providing military support to armed groups and to respect fully the arms embargo; and calls for this withdrawal and the cessation of such support to be monitored and verified through the relevant regional mechanisms, including under the Doha process and the merged Luanda and Nairobi processes;

7. Welcomes the announcement by M23 of its intention to withdraw from Uvira and demands its immediate and faithful implementation, and further demands that M23 and all other armed groups release all children and abducted persons, cease attacks against civilians and humanitarian personnel, and allow unhindered humanitarian access, and requests MONUSCO, within its mandate and in coordination with regional verification mechanisms, to verify and report on the withdrawal of M23 from Uvira, Sange and all other occupied areas;

8. Calls upon the DRC and Rwanda to implement fully and in good faith the peace agreement signed in Washington, D.C., on 4 December 2025, the Regional Economic Integration Framework of 7 November 2025, the Doha agreement of 14 October 2025 and the merged Luanda and Nairobi processes, as well as relevant decisions of the African Union Peace and Security Council, including through the neutralization of the FDLR, the withdrawal of external forces, the establishment and effective functioning of oversight and verification mechanisms and the pursuit of durable political solutions;

9. Expresses its full support for African-led and regionally owned mediation and facilitation efforts, in particular those of Angola, the African Union, the International Conference on the Great Lakes Region, the Southern African Development Community, the East African Community and the Economic Community of Central African States, as well as the efforts of Qatar, the United States and the United Nations, and encourages their continuation and close coordination, including with the African Common Position;

10. Demands that all parties respect and protect civilians, United Nations personnel and peacekeepers, humanitarian personnel, medical personnel, hospitals and other medical facilities, schools and camps for internally displaced persons and refugees, in accordance with their obligations under international humanitarian law and international human rights law, and stresses the need to ensure accountability for all violations and abuses, including attacks against United Nations personnel, conflict-related sexual violence and the recruitment and use of children, and to ensure survivor-centred access to justice, health and psychosocial services for survivors of conflict-related sexual violence and gender-based violence;

11. Calls for the full, equal, meaningful and safe participation of women and youth in peace negotiations, political processes, stabilization efforts and decision-making related to the DRC and the Great Lakes region, and calls upon all actors to strengthen the protection of children and support their rehabilitation and reintegration;

12. Condemns the illicit exploitation and trade of natural resources and all violations of the arms embargo and sanctions regime, calls upon all States to implement fully the measures of the sanctions regime and to cooperate with the Group of Experts, emphasizes that the sanctions regime and the Group of Experts remain essential tools to address violence and destabilization in the eastern DRC, requests the Group of Experts to continue to track financial resources linked to the illicit exploitation and trade of natural resources and to report on such links to armed groups, and expresses its readiness to consider further measures, including through the Committee established pursuant to resolution 1533 (2004), against those who continue to undermine peace and security in the DRC;

13. Calls upon the Committee established pursuant to resolution 1533 (2004) to consider, as a matter of urgency and on the basis of clear and sufficient evidence and in accordance with established procedures and the Committee’s guidelines, designations of individuals and entities responsible for destabilizing activities in the DRC, including M23 leaders and those providing support to M23, and requests the Group of Experts to report on implementation;

14. Urges Member States and international partners to provide adequate humanitarian assistance, support the equitable sharing of the burden and responsibility for hosting refugees, and provide support to the DRC in strengthening its defence and security forces, State authority, rule of law and security sector reform, in full compliance with international law, including international human rights law and international humanitarian law, and with the sanctions regime, including the arms embargo, and with robust human rights vetting, due diligence, oversight and accountability mechanisms, in line with the primary responsibility of the Government of the DRC to protect civilians;

15. Calls for the creation of conditions conducive to the safe, voluntary and dignified return and reintegration of internally displaced persons and refugees, in line with relevant international law and in coordination with the Government of the DRC and regional partners;

16. Requests the Secretary-General to continue to report to the Council every three months, with the first report by 20 March 2026, on the implementation of this resolution, including the security situation in the eastern DRC, the implementation of ceasefire and peace agreements, humanitarian access and the humanitarian situation, the protection of civilians, human rights and international humanitarian law, the implementation of the women, peace and security and youth, peace and security agendas, child protection, climate-related security risks, and the implementation of MONUSCO’s conditions-based withdrawal, and to include recommendations for the adaptation of the Mission’s mandate as appropriate;

17. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.
